  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "JJ has a binary string SSS of length NNN. JJ can perform the following operation on SSS:\n\n\nSelect an iii such that 1≤i≤N1 \\le i \\le N1≤i≤N, and flip SiS_iSi​ (i.e. change 000 to 111 and 111 to 000)\nJJ wants to minimize the number of inversions in SSS by performing the above operation **at most** KKK times. Can you help JJ do so?\n\nRecall that a pair of indices (i,j)(i, j)(i,j) in SSS is called an inversion if i&lt;ji \\lt ji&lt;j and Si&gt;SjS_i \\gt S_jSi​&gt;Sj​.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line contains a single integer TTT - the number of test cases. Then the test cases follow.\nThe first line of each test case contains two integers NNN and KKK - the length of the binary string SSS and the maximum number of times JJ can perform the given operation.\nThe second line of each test case contains a binary string SSS of length NNN containing 000s and 111s only.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output the minimum number of inversions possible after performing the given operation at most KKK times.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N≤1051 \\leq N \\leq 10^51≤N≤105",
      "0≤K≤N0 \\leq K \\leq N0≤K≤N",
      "Sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 2⋅1052 \\cdot 10^52⋅105"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n8 3\n00010111\n5 1\n10100\n6 2\n111000",
        "output": "0\n2\n3",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 1: We are allowed to perform at most 333 operations. We can perform the following operation on SSS: 00010‾111→000111110001\\underline{0}111 \\rightarrow 0001111100010​111→00011111 which has 000 inversions. Therefore 000 is the answer.\nTest case 2: We can perform the following operation on SSS: 1‾0100→00100\\underline{1}0100 \\rightarrow 001001​0100→00100 which has 222 inversions. It can be proven that this is the minimum number of inversions possible.\nTest case 3: We can perform the following operations on SSS: 11100‾0→111010‾→1110111110\\underline{0}0 \\rightarrow 11101\\underline{0} \\rightarrow 11101111100​0→111010​→111011 which has 333 inversions. It can be proven that this is the minimum number of inversions possible."
      }
    ]
  },
